# laguna-escondida telemetry sidecar (Grafana Alloy) — configuration TEMPLATE.
#
# Copy this file to  env\alloy.env  and fill in the real values, then run
# scripts\install.ps1. install.ps1 reads this file and:
#   - exports every LAGUNA_* var below as a MACHINE environment variable, which the
#     laguna-alloy service inherits (alloy\config.alloy reads them with sys.env),
#   - writes LAGUNA_NODE_KEY to  env\alloy-node-key  (gitignored, ACL'd to
#     SYSTEM+Administrators) and points LAGUNA_NODE_KEY_FILE at it, so the key is
#     NOT readable from every other process's environment on the box,
#   - registers + starts the laguna-alloy service.
#
# If env\alloy.env does NOT exist, install.ps1 skips the telemetry sidecar entirely
# and the box runs exactly as it did before. That is the supported "telemetry off"
# state — there is nothing to uninstall.
#
# This .example IS committed; env\alloy.env is GITIGNORED. Never commit real secrets.
#
# >>> This box holds NO Grafana Cloud credentials. <<<
# The cloud ingest owns them and stamps the tenant context. All this box needs is the
# ingest address and its own node key — the same key the data-sync path already uses.

# --- Where telemetry goes (required) -------------------------------------------
# Base URL of the CLOUD BACKEND's telemetry ingest — not Grafana Cloud. The exporter
# appends /v1/logs, /v1/traces and /v1/metrics itself, so do NOT include those here.
LAGUNA_TELEMETRY_INGEST_URL=https://api.example.com/api/telemetry

# The node credential, sent as the X-Node-Key header. MUST be the same value as
# NODE_SYNC_KEY in env\edge-node.env — the cloud matches them against one key.
LAGUNA_NODE_KEY=CHANGE_ME

# --- Per-box identity (required) -----------------------------------------------
# Stamped onto scraped metrics so they carry the same identity the app already puts
# on its own logs and traces. MUST match NODE_ID / ORGANIZATION_ID in
# env\edge-node.env, or metrics and logs will not line up under one box in Grafana.
LAGUNA_NODE_ID=
LAGUNA_ORGANIZATION_ID=
# LAGUNA_DEPLOYMENT_MODE=edge
# LAGUNA_ENVIRONMENT=production
# LAGUNA_SERVICE_NAME=laguna-backend      # must match OTEL_SERVICE_NAME in edge-node.env

# --- Volume controls ------------------------------------------------------------
# Lowest log severity forwarded to the cloud. DEBUG and TRACE are dropped at INFO.
# One of: TRACE | DEBUG | INFO | WARN | ERROR | FATAL.
# LAGUNA_MIN_LOG_SEVERITY=INFO

# Hard cap on the disk buffer, in BYTES. Default 33554432 (32 MiB).
#
# BUDGET ~6x THIS NUMBER OF DISK. The cap is applied PER SIGNAL (separate buffer
# files for logs, traces and metrics) and the underlying bbolt file allocates in
# doublings, so 32 MiB was measured holding a ~64 MiB file per signal — about
# 192 MiB worst case for the three together.
#
# When the buffer is full Alloy discards NEW telemetry and keeps what is already
# buffered, so a very long outage keeps its beginning and loses its tail.
# LAGUNA_TELEMETRY_BUFFER_BYTES=33554432

# How long a failing batch keeps being retried before it is dropped. Set well above
# a realistic outage so an ordinary offline stretch is delivered in full.
# LAGUNA_TELEMETRY_MAX_RETRY_AGE=24h

# --- Local endpoints ------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults match what the edge node already uses; change only if you moved a port.
# LAGUNA_OTLP_GRPC_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:4317     # OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT points here
# LAGUNA_OTLP_HTTP_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:4318
# LAGUNA_METRICS_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:9090       # edge-node METRICS_PORT
# LAGUNA_METRICS_SCRAPE_INTERVAL=60s
# LAGUNA_METRICS_SCRAPE_TIMEOUT=10s           # MUST stay below the interval
# LAGUNA_BATCH_TIMEOUT=10s

# --- Buffer/diagnostics paths ---------------------------------------------------
# install.ps1 defaults this to <install root>\alloy-data\buffer.
# LAGUNA_TELEMETRY_BUFFER_DIR=C:\laguna-edge\alloy-data\buffer

# Alloy's own log level (debug|info|warn|error). Its logs go to logs\alloy.*.log and
# are never forwarded to the cloud.
# LAGUNA_ALLOY_LOG_LEVEL=warn
